# What must NOT go into the image build context.
#
# This file is not only about size. `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile means every file here would otherwise
# be shipped into a layer, and this repository's own `.gitignore` documents two categories that must
# never leave the machine: a real `.env` holding live API keys, and `~/.myriaxon` state. The build
# context is sent to the Docker daemon BEFORE any instruction runs, so a secret in the context has
# already left the machine even if no later step copies it.
#
# `.dockerignore` is therefore a security control here, not a build optimisation.

# --- secrets, first because they matter most -------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.env.bak
.myriaxon/
.iohcode/

# --- version control and CI --------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.git` is the largest thing in most build contexts and the only part that is genuinely useless
# inside: a full object store, often larger than the source. The Dockerfile needs no history -- the
# build revision arrives as a LABEL argument instead.
.git/
.gitattributes
.github/

# --- what the image does not need --------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests, docs and packaging config are for people working ON the project, not for running it. The
# built-in skills and IQB-14 task suite are NOT here: `package-data` ships them in the wheel, so
# excluding the source directory would remove them from the installed copy.
tests/
docs/
bench-out/
redteam/
posters/
brand/
docker/

# ...EXCEPT the entrypoint, which lives in docker/ but is part of the application image rather than the
# sandbox build. The `docker/` rule above is there to keep the SANDBOX Dockerfile (and its apt package
# list) out of the application context; without this exception the entrypoint is excluded too, and the
# image either fails to build or silently gets a stale copy. The negation has to come after the rule it
# undoes -- `.dockerignore` applies patterns in order, and the last match wins, exactly like .gitignore.
!docker/entrypoint.py

# --- build and cache artefacts -----------------------------------------------------------------
# A stale local `build/` would be copied in and `pip install .` could resolve the OLD tree, which is
# how an image silently contains the previous commit.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
venv/

# --- editor and OS noise -----------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- anything a previous run left behind -------------------------------------------------------
# Generated output and scratch files. `*-proof.txt` and `ANKER_*.md` are in `.gitignore` for the same
# reason: a Locus working in this repository writes documents here, and they are not source.
*.log
*.bundle
ANKER_*.md
*-proof.txt
task.md
